# grammar: v0 (unfrozen; AST normative)
#
# runes — the language layer of the Norse world (05). Names and words are world
# knowledge, so a dialect lives in the fabric like any other lore: statused and
# forkable. This pack declares the Old Norse tongue as a dialect with its
# phonotactic frame and a dithematic name grammar, composes canonical hero-names
# as TYPED DERIVATIONS (each checked at compile: slot sequence, role membership,
# phonotactics), coins a word and a term, and shows the agent-init flow — a
# proposal bundle under one hypothesis, ratified in one recorded act.
scope runes

# --- the pack's ledger --------------------------------------------------------
ratify d-0201   # the dialect and its cited canon name-parts
ratify d-0202   # the canon hero-names
ratify d-0203   # the coined word and term
ratify d-0205   # the ratify block that settles the loan-word bundle

# --- the dialect as a declaration (05 §1) -------------------------------------
# Old Norse is dithematic: a hero-name is a proto + deutero compound (Sig-run,
# Thor-ulf). `basis attested` is a claim about OUR world (is this pattern Norse-
# like?), graded and replayable but NEVER a support variable — the author ratifies
# the SOUND, not the scholarship (the attestation seam, 05 §7).
dialect old-norse "the Old Norse tongue"  decided d-0201 {
  basis attested "Old Norse / Old West Norse"
  vowels a e i o u y
  endings -r -i -a -unn
  cluster-max 2
  stress initial
  name-kind person.given = given-proto given-deutero
  name-part sig  "victory"          role given-proto    decided d-0201
  name-part thor "the thunder-god"  role given-proto    decided d-0201
  name-part gud  "god"              role given-proto    decided d-0201
  name-part run  "rune, secret"     role given-deutero  decided d-0201
  name-part ulf  "wolf"             role given-deutero  decided d-0201
  name-part mund "hand, protection" role given-deutero  decided d-0201
  }

# --- names as typed derivations: dialect-true is a compile verdict (05 §2) ----
name sigrun "Sigrun"  person.given in old-norse
  = given-proto sig + given-deutero run
  bearer the-seeress   decided d-0202
  # checked at compile: the slot sequence [given-proto, given-deutero] matches the
  # name-kind pattern, each name-part exists under its claimed role, and the
  # composed surface passes the phonotactics. A mispronunciation IS a failed
  # derivation (the seidr practice's L4 mislaid-working, one fabric).

name thorulf "Thorulf"  person.given in old-norse
  = given-proto thor + given-deutero ulf
  bearer the-wanderer   decided d-0202

name gudmund "Gudmund"  person.given in old-norse
  = given-proto gud + given-deutero mund
  bearer heimdall   decided d-0202

# --- the lexicon: a coined word and a diegetic term (05 §4) -------------------
word runestave "rune-stave"  = given-deutero run   decided d-0203
term the-elder-futhark "the four-and-twenty carved staves by which fate is read and worked"   decided d-0203

# --- the agent-init flow: a proposal bundle under one hypothesis (05 §5) -------
# Candidate dialect data enters like world lore: proposed, under one hypothesis
# variable, every clause carrying agent provenance by inheritance. Composed through
# an unratified name-part, the name is merely-possible BY CONSTRUCTION — visible to
# shadow queries, structurally excluded from every context pack and assertion until
# the bundle is ruled. Here the author HAS ruled: one valuation ratifies the bundle
# and the loan-name flips from possible to certain.
hypothesis h-loan-name "the Norse tongue took a hero-name from the tongue over the sea"

name-part har "grey, hoary"  role given-proto  proposed agent:factory/naming-run-0721  assumes h-loan-name

name harmund "Harmund"  person.given in old-norse
  = given-proto har + given-deutero mund
  proposed agent:factory/naming-run-0721  assumes h-loan-name
  # a rejected name-part would valuate false and this name would die into the
  # killed-token dialect; instead the ruling below settles the bundle true.

ratify d-0205 { h-loan-name := true }   # one valuation ratifies the whole bundle
